## Investment moves: ranked, with evidence

**Answer: do move 1.** Move $63,647 above your reserve to a higher-rate safe account you can withdraw in time (first do the steps in move 1). Gain until you need it: $60 to $120 a year. *Not personal advice: you decide.*

*Education and arithmetic on your own data, not personal financial advice. This assistant is not a licensed financial advisor. No stock picks, no market timing: every gain below is a range from long-run planning assumptions, not a forecast. No provider pays for any recommendation. The ranking is fixed rules in code; chat explanations come from an AI model and can be wrong.*

Question any move: say "challenge `<id>`" (skill `invest-challenge`). Override one in profile.toml under `[[invest.overrides]]` with `move`, `reason` and an optional `until` date; the plan respects it.

| # | Move | Long-run average gain a year (range, not next year) | One-time cost | Task |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Keep the $63,647 above your reserve safe, at the best rate (needed in about 10 months) (`cash-drag`) | $60 to $120 a year | — | yes |
| 2 | Move $14 from your taxable account into TFSA room (`fill-room`) | $0 to $0 a year | — | no |
| 3 | Do nothing (`do-nothing`) | $0 (baseline) | — | no |

### 1. Keep the $63,647 above your reserve safe, at the best rate (needed in about 10 months) (`cash-drag`)

- **Do:** Move $63,647 above your reserve to a higher-rate safe account you can withdraw in time; if it is for a first home, put up to $8,000 of it in your FHSA (First Home Savings Account), in savings you can withdraw in time inside it
- **Gain vs. doing nothing:** $60 to $120 a year.
- **Why:** You need this money within 10 months, too soon for stocks. A high-interest account, GIC (a deposit with a fixed rate and term) or cash ETF pays about 2.4% versus your 2%. If it is for a first home, up to $8,000 of it can sit in your FHSA in the same safe holdings: the contribution is deductible (about $3,473 less tax once, at your marginal rate (the tax on your next dollar of income)) and a withdrawal for the home is tax-free.
- **Risk:** GICs lock money until maturity; cash ETFs are not CDIC (the federal deposit insurance)-insured.
- **Assumes:** After tax at your marginal rate, earned for about 10 months, not a full year. Safe rates near 2.4% (planning assumption; low end half of the gap).
- **Assumes:** The FHSA tip applies only if this money is for a first home.
- **Strongest objection:** Markets can fall about 42% in a bad year (estimate, `one-year-drop`); money invested now may be needed before it recovers.
- **Evidence:**
  - `short-horizon` (heuristic): Money you need within about 5 years (a down payment, say) stays in safe places: high-interest savings, GICs or cash ETFs. Stocks can take years to recover from a fall. [source](https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/savings-investments/investing-basics.html), as of 2026-11-01, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `cash-rate-capture` (heuristic): Low end of the gain from a safe account: assume you capture only half the gap between the best safe rate and your current rate (rates change, money moves late). A rule of thumb used only for the low end of a range. No external source: a judgment call, as of 2026-11-09, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `one-year-drop` (estimate): Global stocks can fall about 40% in one calendar year: the MSCI ACWI lost about 42% (net, USD) in 2008. Money you need within a few years does not belong in stocks. [source](https://www.msci.com/documents/1296102/1636401/ACWI+factsheet/d5d4e6b0-3957-4938-a4aa-dbc956ae93af), as of 2026-11-01, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `pag-short-term` (verified): Long-run planning assumption for cash and short-term deposits: 2.4% a year. [source](https://www.fpcanada.ca/newsdetail/fp-canada--and-the-institute-of-financial-planning-jointly-publish-the-2026-projection-assumption-guidelines-for-financial-planners), as of 2026-04-16, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `fhsa` (verified): A First Home Savings Account gives a first-time home buyer $8,000 of room in the first year, then $8,000 a year; unused room carries forward, but only up to $8,000, so the most you can add in one year is $16,000, up to $40,000 for life. Room starts only once the account is open. Contributions are generally deductible, and a qualifying withdrawal to buy a first home is tax-free when all its conditions are met. [source](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/topics/first-home-savings-account.html), as of 2026-11-09, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `tax-rules` (verified): Canada personal income tax 2026: capital gains inclusion rate and TFSA (Tax-Free Savings Account), RRSP (Registered Retirement Savings Plan) and FHSA limits. [source](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/tax-rates-brackets/current-year.html), as of 2026-10-19, review by 2027-01-31.
  - `investing-data` (verified): Fund fees (MERs) and tickers in knowledge/data/investing.toml. [source](https://www.blackrock.com/ca/investors/en/products/309480/ishares-core-equity-etf-portfolio), as of 2026-10-19, review by 2027-04-30.

### 2. Move $14 from your taxable account into TFSA room (`fill-room`)

- **Gain vs. doing nothing:** $0 to $0 a year.
- **Why:** Growth in a TFSA is never taxed; in a taxable account it is.
- **Risk:** None from markets (same investments, new account). Room used now comes back only the next calendar year after a withdrawal.
- **Cost:** Selling first realizes gains; their tax is unknown without each holding's market price ([invest.prices]).
- **Assumes:** Your mix returns 3.8% to 7.8% a year; your marginal rate stays 43.4%.
- **Assumes:** Low end: gains deferred for years. High end: gains realized every year.
- **Strongest objection:** Registered room never expires; moving money in now only helps if you will not need it back soon. Weakest evidence: `return-band` is a heuristic only.
- **Evidence:**
  - `tfsa-shelter` (verified): Growth in a TFSA is never taxed. In a non-registered account, half of each realized gain is taxed at your marginal rate and interest is fully taxed every year. [source](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/forms-publications/publications/rc4466/tax-free-savings-account-tfsa-guide-individuals.html), as of 2026-10-19, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `pag-equity` (verified): Long-run planning assumption for a global all-equity mix: about 6.5% a year before fees. By region: Canadian stocks 6.3%, US stocks 6.4%, international developed stocks 6.6%, emerging-market stocks 7.5%. A mix like VEQT (about 46% US, 30% Canada, 18% international developed, 7% emerging, Sep 30 2026) blends to 6.5%. For projections of 10+ years, not a forecast for any one year. [source](https://www.fpcanada.ca/newsdetail/fp-canada--and-the-institute-of-financial-planning-jointly-publish-the-2026-projection-assumption-guidelines-for-financial-planners), as of 2026-11-14, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `return-band` (heuristic): Width of the range around each return assumption: plus or minus 2 points a year. Nobody knows long-run returns; this wide band is a judgment call to keep ranges honest, not a sourced number. No external source: a judgment call, as of 2026-11-01, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `pag-fixed-income` (verified): Long-run planning assumption for bonds: 3.2% a year before fees. [source](https://www.fpcanada.ca/newsdetail/fp-canada--and-the-institute-of-financial-planning-jointly-publish-the-2026-projection-assumption-guidelines-for-financial-planners), as of 2026-04-16, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `gain-deferral-share` (heuristic): Low end of yearly tax drag on stock gains in a taxable account: about half of the gains are taxed each year when most gains are deferred for years. A rule of thumb used only for the low end of a range. No external source: a judgment call, as of 2026-11-09, review by 2027-04-30.
  - `tax-rules` (verified): Canada personal income tax 2026: capital gains inclusion rate and TFSA, RRSP and FHSA limits. [source](https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/individuals/tax-rates-brackets/current-year.html), as of 2026-10-19, review by 2027-01-31.
- Below your simplicity threshold ($50 a year): no task.

### 3. Do nothing (`do-nothing`)

- **Gain vs. doing nothing:** $0 (baseline).
- **Why:** Zero effort, zero trading cost, no new tax. It is the baseline every move must beat.
- **Risk:** Your money keeps today's risks: the $63,647 above your reserve earns -0.1% a year after inflation (-$64).
- **Strongest objection:** Idle cash and drift compound quietly; doing nothing for years has a real cost.
- **Evidence:**
  - `pag-inflation` (verified): Long-run planning assumption for inflation: 2.1% a year. [source](https://www.fpcanada.ca/newsdetail/fp-canada--and-the-institute-of-financial-planning-jointly-publish-the-2026-projection-assumption-guidelines-for-financial-planners), as of 2026-04-16, review by 2027-04-30.

